    <h1>How Fast Can Ravens Fly?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven cruises at roughly 25–50 km/h in level flight and can reach approximately 80 km/h (50 mph) or more in a dive. Speed is not what distinguishes ravens as fliers — it is their aerial agility. Ravens are exceptional soaring birds and perform complex aerobatics during courtship and social displays that few birds of similar size can match.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Flight Speed: The Numbers</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Precise measurement of wild bird flight speed is methodologically difficult. Published figures for Common Ravens draw on radar tracking, wind-tunnel experiments, and field observations:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cruise speed (level flight):</strong> approximately 25–50 km/h. The lower end represents relaxed travel; sustained powered flight runs closer to 40–50 km/h.</li>
      <li><strong>Maximum speed (dive/powered descent):</strong> approximately 80 km/h or more. Figures above 100 km/h are sometimes cited but are not well-supported by systematic measurement.</li>
      <li><strong>Soaring:</strong> in strong thermal conditions, ravens can maintain altitude at slow speeds of 15–25 km/h while expending minimal energy — soaring economy comparable to large raptors.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For comparison: Peregrine Falcons dive at 300+ km/h; Golden Eagles cruise at 50–70 km/h; American Crows cruise at around 40–50 km/h. Ravens are solidly fast but unremarkable among large birds in terms of straight-line speed.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Are Famous For: Aerial Agility</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The most striking aspect of raven flight is not speed but manoeuvrability. Ravens are among the most aerobatically accomplished of large birds:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Courtship displays:</strong> breeding pairs perform complex aerial displays — soaring together, diving, tumbling, and rolling; pairs sometimes fly in tight synchrony with wingtips nearly touching.</li>
      <li><strong>Play flight:</strong> young ravens and non-breeding flocks engage in extensive aerial play — barrel rolls, inverted flight, diving, chasing, and object-dropping. This is documented in both wild and captive birds.</li>
      <li><strong>Wind riding:</strong> at cliff edges and mountain ridges, ravens exploit turbulent wind to perform prolonged aerial acrobatics — hovering, side-slipping, and tumbling in strong gusts.</li>
      <li><strong>Mobbing and evasion:</strong> ravens regularly mob much larger raptors (eagles, buzzards) and can outmanoeuvre them in tight aerial combat due to superior agility at lower speeds.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Wing Shape and Flight Style</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven's wings are long and broad — wingspan 115–150 cm — with deeply fingered primary feathers ("fingers") at the tip. This wing shape is adapted for:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Efficient soaring and gliding — broad wings generate high lift at low airspeeds.</li>
      <li>Powerful flapping flight — relatively long arm and hand allow high stroke amplitude.</li>
      <li>Manoeuvrability — the ability to adjust individual primary feathers gives fine control over lift distribution.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The characteristic wedge-shaped tail (a key identification feature) also contributes to aerial control, acting as a rudder in tight manoeuvres.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/questions/how-big-are-ravens/">How big are ravens?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/">Raven vs Crow</a></li>
      <li><a href="/species/common-raven/">Common Raven — species profile</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

